# Target C — exact projected stress dimension and reduced reinsertion burden

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** proof-only bridge between audited-working §156 and the stress-transversal reinsertion theorem; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The stress-transversal reinsertion report proves that deleting at most `s_Z+1` core
vertices kills every normalized mixed KKT stress, and remarks that the same argument
may instead use the affine dimension of the **projected diameter-stress polytope**.
That projected dimension was left abstract.

Audited-working §156 determines it exactly.

Let

\[
M=R_{C\cup H},
\qquad
\mathcal L:=\ker M^T
\]

be the full mixed self-stress space on the connected nonisolated positive-contact core.
Canonical mixed rank gives

\[
\dim\mathcal L=s_Z+1.
\tag{PD.1}
\]

Let

\[
\sigma_C:=\dim\ker R_C^T.
\]

Audited-working §156 gives

\[
\sigma_C=2f-\operatorname{rank}C_{face},
\qquad
0\le\sigma_C\le s_Z.
\tag{PD.2}
\]

Write `pi_H(alpha,beta)=beta` for projection to the actual-diameter coordinates.

---

## 1. Exact dimension of the diameter-stress image

### Theorem PD.1

The linear image of the full mixed stress space in diameter coordinates has dimension

\[
\boxed{
\dim \pi_H(\mathcal L)=s_Z+1-\sigma_C.
}
\tag{PD.3}
\]

The normalized projected stress polytope

\[
\mathcal P_H
:=\left\{\beta:\exists\alpha\ge0,
\ (\alpha,\beta)\in\mathcal L,
\ \beta\ge0,
\ \sum_H\beta=1\right\}
\]

has affine dimension

\[
\boxed{
\dim\operatorname{aff}\mathcal P_H
=s_Z-\sigma_C.
}
\tag{PD.4}
\]

### Proof

The kernel of `pi_H` restricted to `mathcal L` consists exactly of mixed self-stresses
with zero diameter part:

\[
\ker(\pi_H|_{\mathcal L})
=\{(\alpha,0):R_C^T\alpha=0\}.
\]

Its dimension is `sigma_C`. Rank-nullity with (PD.1) gives (PD.3).

Put

\[
W:=\pi_H(\mathcal L).
\]

The normalization functional

\[
s(\beta):=\sum_H\beta
\]

is nonzero on `W`, because the canonical relative-interior KKT stress has strictly
positive actual-diameter coordinates and is normalized by `s(beta)=1`.
Therefore

\[
W_1:=W\cap\{s=1\}
\]

is a nonempty affine hyperplane in `W`, of dimension

\[
\dim W-1=s_Z-\sigma_C.
\tag{PD.5}
\]

Let

\[
\mathcal A
:=\{(\alpha,\beta)\in\mathcal L:s(\beta)=1\}.
\]

The canonical full-support relative-interior KKT stress has every contact and diameter
coordinate strictly positive. Hence the nonnegativity inequalities do not reduce the
affine hull of the normalized stress polytope: its affine hull is exactly `mathcal A`.
Linear projection commutes with affine hull, so

\[
\operatorname{aff}\mathcal P_H
=\pi_H(\mathcal A)
=W_1.
\]

Equation (PD.4) follows from (PD.5). `square`

---

## 2. Reduced stress transversal

The finite face-of-a-polytope argument from the stress-transversal reinsertion theorem
can now be run directly on `mathcal P_H`.

### Corollary PD.2

There is a set `J` of actual diameter edges with

\[
\boxed{
|J|\le s_Z-\sigma_C+1
}
\tag{PD.6}
\]

such that no normalized mixed KKT stress vanishes on every diameter coordinate in
`J`.

Choosing one endpoint of each edge in `J` gives a core vertex set `S` with

\[
\boxed{
|S|\le s_Z-\sigma_C+1
}
\tag{PD.7}
\]

whose deletion removes every normalized inherited mixed stress. Consequently the
protected-reinsertion theorem needs packing reserve only

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{reserve}(W,Z)
\ge s_Z-\sigma_C+1,
}
\tag{PD.8}
\]

rather than the earlier sufficient threshold `s_Z+1`.

### Proof

Apply the nested-face argument to the compact polytope `mathcal P_H`, whose affine
dimension is (PD.4). At most `d+1=s_Z-sigma_C+1` coordinate-zero cuts make the
intersection empty. If the selected diameter edges are removed by deleting one chosen
endpoint of each, any normalized stress on the retained source would extend by zeros
to an original normalized stress whose projected diameter vector lies in the forbidden
coordinate face. This is impossible.

The guarded-reinsertion theorem then applies verbatim with the smaller deletion set.
`square`

---

## 3. Outer-train form of the same bound

Let `L_OO` be the number of complete stopped trains whose two terminal occurrences lie
on the outer face. Audited-working §156 gives

\[
\operatorname{rank}C_{face}\le T-L_{OO}
\]

and the exact train ledger

\[
T=h-s_Z+2f.
\]

Using (PD.2),

\[
\begin{aligned}
s_Z-\sigma_C
&=s_Z-2f+\operatorname{rank}C_{face}\\
&\le s_Z-2f+T-L_{OO}\\
&=h-L_{OO}.
\end{aligned}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\dim\operatorname{aff}\mathcal P_H
=s_Z-\sigma_C
\le h-L_{OO},
}
\tag{PD.9}
\]

and one may choose

\[
\boxed{
|S|\le h-L_{OO}+1.
}
\tag{PD.10}
\]

This is a useful complement to §157: many outer-to-outer trains not only impose signed
diameter bracing; they also reduce the number of core vertices that must be teleported
before a strict retained-core diameter descent exists.

---

## 4. Exact special cases

1. If `sigma_C=s_Z`, then the projected normalized diameter-stress polytope is a single
   point and **one** selected diameter endpoint suffices to destroy all normalized
   stresses.
2. If `sigma_C=0`, the theorem reduces to the old `s_Z+1` bound.
3. If `L_OO=h`, then (PD.9) forces projected dimension zero, again reducing the
   reinsertion burden to one point.

No assertion is made that any of these endpoint cases must occur in the inactive
chamber branch.

---

## 5. Scope and frontier effect

- The theorem is on the nonisolated positive-contact core only. Contact-isolated source
  points remain in the full source and are not inserted into the rigidity matrix.
- The deleted vertices are restored through guarded reinsertion before any global
  contradiction is claimed.
- The theorem uses the full-support relative-interior canonical KKT stress to identify
  the affine hull; it does not assume a uniform positive multiplier floor.
- The exact identity is linear algebra. The stress-transversal/reinsertion mechanism is
  still proof-only and remains conditional on finding enough protected packing reserve.
- In the macroscopic inactive-container branch the missing problem is now sharper:
  construct only `s_Z-sigma_C+1` protected extra packing sites, or couple the chamber
  geometry to show that this reduced burden is impossible to sustain.

**Disposition:** proof-only structural bridge. C-II remains open.